The spring of ninety-four was a shadow of dread,  
The paddock was quiet with thoughts of the dead.  
For Barrichello had crashed, and Ratzenberger fell,  
Turning San Marino to a terrifying hell.  
Ayrton stood in the garage, his spirit unstrung,  
As the dark bells of caution and destiny rung.

On the first day of May, under clear, heavy skies,  
He climbed in the Williams with doubt in his eyes.  
The green and yellow helmet was buckled in place,  
To lead the great field in a phantom-like race.  
The lights flashed to green and the engines roared loud,  
As the line of machines tore away from the crowd.

Lap seven arrived, and the speed was sublime,  
A desperate struggle with tarmac and time.  
At three hundred kilometers, breaking the wind,  
The steering column snapped and the universe sinned.  
At the Tamburello curve, where the concrete was bare,  
The champion’s car drifted out of the air.

× × ×

The impact was thunder, the blue car was rent,  
A sudden, cruel silence where energy spent.  
The wheel came spinning, a devastating blow,  
As the red-and-white flags began weeping below.  
The helicopter rose in the Italian sun,  
But the race of the master already was done.

Inside the crushed cockpit, untouched by the flame,  
They found a small flag that he wished to proclaim:  
The banner of Austria, packed in his sight,  
To honor his fallen comrade in the light.  
The heart of Brazil stopped its beating that day,  
As the soul of the rainmaster floated away.

